{"subjectId":"sql","id":"upsert","title":"UPSERT: Insert or Update","group":"Defining & Changing Data","order":10,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">An <em>upsert</em> inserts a row, or updates it if a row with the same key already exists — atomically, in one statement, without a race between “check” and “write”.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-git-merge\"></i>INSERT … ON CONFLICT (PostgreSQL, SQLite)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>CREATE TABLE inventory (\n    sku        TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\n    qty        INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    updated_on TEXT NOT NULL\n);\nINSERT INTO inventory VALUES ('PEN', 100, '2024-03-01'), ('DESK', 5, '2024-03-01');\n\nINSERT INTO inventory (sku, qty, updated_on) VALUES\n    ('PEN',   50, '2024-03-10'),      -- exists  → add to stock\n    ('CHAIR', 12, '2024-03-10')       -- new     → insert\nON CONFLICT (sku) DO UPDATE\n    SET qty        = inventory.qty + excluded.qty,\n        updated_on = excluded.updated_on;\n\nSELECT * FROM inventory ORDER BY sku;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>sku   | qty | updated_on\n------+-----+-----------\nCHAIR | 12  | 2024-03-10\nDESK  | 5   | 2024-03-01\nPEN   | 150 | 2024-03-10</code></pre></figure>\n<p><code>excluded</code> is the row that <em>would</em> have been inserted; <code>inventory.qty</code> is the existing row. <code>ON CONFLICT (sku) DO NOTHING</code> simply skips duplicates.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-language\"></i>Other dialects</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>MySQL</figcaption><pre><code>INSERT INTO inventory (sku, qty, updated_on) VALUES ('PEN', 50, '2024-03-10')\nON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE\n    qty = qty + VALUES(qty),          -- MySQL 8.0.20+: prefer an alias, e.g. AS new … new.qty\n    updated_on = VALUES(updated_on);</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL Server / Oracle / PostgreSQL 15+ (MERGE)</figcaption><pre><code>MERGE INTO inventory AS t\nUSING (VALUES ('PEN', 50, '2024-03-10')) AS s (sku, qty, updated_on)\n    ON t.sku = s.sku\nWHEN MATCHED THEN\n    UPDATE SET qty = t.qty + s.qty, updated_on = s.updated_on\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN\n    INSERT (sku, qty, updated_on) VALUES (s.sku, s.qty, s.updated_on);</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>The REPLACE trap</h3>\n<p>SQLite's <code>INSERT OR REPLACE</code> (and MySQL's <code>REPLACE</code>) <strong>deletes</strong> the old row and inserts a new one — columns you did not supply fall back to defaults.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>CREATE TABLE users (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT, plan TEXT DEFAULT 'free');\nINSERT INTO users VALUES (1, 'Asha', 'premium');\n\nINSERT OR REPLACE INTO users (id, name) VALUES (1, 'Asha R');   -- plan is lost!\nSELECT * FROM users;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>id | name   | plan\n---+--------+-----\n1  | Asha R | free</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>REPLACE</code> also fires delete triggers and can cascade-delete child rows. Prefer <code>ON CONFLICT … DO UPDATE</code> / <code>ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE</code>, which update in place.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Race condition</div><p>“<code>SELECT</code> then <code>INSERT</code> or <code>UPDATE</code>” in application code is a race: two requests can both see “not found” and both insert. Upsert statements (or a unique constraint plus retry) make it atomic.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you implement an upsert?</summary><div><p>Put a <code>UNIQUE</code>/<code>PRIMARY KEY</code> on the natural key, then use the engine's atomic form: <code>INSERT … ON CONFLICT (key) DO UPDATE SET … = excluded.…</code> (PostgreSQL/SQLite), <code>INSERT … ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE</code> (MySQL), or <code>MERGE</code> (SQL Server/Oracle/PostgreSQL 15+). The conflict target must be backed by a unique index.</p></div></details></section>"}